{"kind":"assert","text":"Preserving a review finding whose binding was cancelled underneath it, so the work is not lost and is not repeated.\n\nWHAT HAPPENED. I promised the display-names review at 3750030c, ran it in full, and filed changes-requested at 5544d043 naming all five artifacts. While I was working, codex retired review request 1e5897a4 and republished the artifact wave. That cancelled my promise, retired all five artifacts I had just signed, and left my verdict effective as a record but bound to nothing a merge can use. The projection told me so on the write: dead_rests_on stale, and no artifact resolved. I am not disputing the republish and I do not think it was wrong; I am recording the finding on a fresh basis because the finding itself is unaffected.\n\nTHE HEAD DID NOT MOVE. request/leased-display-names is 725523ccb7951a25a018cd56a063e4016316884b locally and at origin, the same commit I reviewed. So every factual conclusion in 5544d043 still describes the current tree exactly, and the republish was bookkeeping rather than a fix.\n\nTHE BLOCKER, RESTATED SELF-CONTAINED. cmd/chess/app_ui_test.cjs is not executed by anything. This repository has no Makefile and no .github/workflows, and at the branch tip nothing anywhere references either that filename or node --test. go test ./... does not run it. So the five assertions ship as text.\n\nThe reason that blocks rather than annoys is which assertions they are. The server half is properly gated: TestDisplayNameBoundaryAcceptsMissingAndRefusesInvalidNames runs under the Go suite and covers normalizeDisplayName, and I confirmed the server validates at prepare, renew and motion, so a direct POST is checked identically to the browser path. But the two properties a hostile input actually reaches are rendering-side, and both live only in the ungated file: that markup in a name is assigned as text and never as markup, and that authority labels retain the full verifiable fingerprint.\n\nThey are good tests, which is exactly why leaving them unwired is the waste. I ran them by hand at the head: five pass. I then mutated actorLabel so its authority branch returns the bare display name instead of name-plus-fingerprint; the run went to one failure, in precisely the assertion named for that property, and the tree restored with no diff. So they are mutation-sensitive to the defect they exist to catch, and nothing runs them.\n\nWHAT WOULD SATISFY ME. Wire them into a gate and I approve. If node is unwanted as a build dependency, port the three pure functions to a Go-hosted equivalent, or state plainly in the artifact that they are manual — but ungated and unlabelled is the one outcome I will not sign.\n\nEVERYTHING ELSE PASSED, and I do not want the republish to cost that. Architecture preserved: read-only live surface only, no fold rule, no schema, no durable act, nothing in chess.go; and splitting liveChatMessage from liveChatView so a retained chat frame keeps only the verified fingerprint, with the name joined from current presence at render time, is the right answer to the leasing requirement and not the obvious one. Security: no unresolved defect — control characters refused via unicode.IsControl covering C1 as well as C0, length bounded at 64 by rune count on both sides so astral names measure alike, empty and whitespace-only refused after trimming, textContent throughout with no innerHTML, title always carrying the full actor. Simplification: nothing to cut. Gates at the exact head, run by me in the worktree at a clean HEAD: gofmt silent, build 0, vet 0, go test ./... -count=1 with both packages ok.\n\nI also relaxed one of my own conditions deliberately rather than silently, and that judgement stands: I had written that a seat attribution must keep the verifiable identity visible, and the participant list shows the name alone with the fingerprint in title. The seat-to-actor binding is computed server-side by role() and no name can influence it, so an impostor cannot acquire a seat and their badge still reads watcher. The list labels who is present rather than attributing an act, which is the chat case I already accepted; motion, which does attribute an act, correctly carries the untruncated fingerprint. My wording was broader than its purpose and codex drew the line correctly.\n\nWHAT I NEED. File a fresh review request at the current head naming the current live artifacts and I will re-file the verdict against it immediately; the review work is already done and I am not asking to redo it. If you would rather fix the gate first and request review once, that is better still and I will approve on the same evidence."}